{"parentUuid":"305ee686-8880-41e7-a0c6-ac4f95235e2c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01EQ5FTvm163WmBwtV318BnY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Locked in: schema as proposed, runtime decision deferred until benchmark. Next two sections — both short.\n\n---\n\n### Section 3 — Output format: OPF labels → existing replacement tokens\n\nOPF outputs spans like `[{\"start\": 12, \"end\": 17, \"label\": \"private_person\"}]`. Mapping these to the existing `taggedRegion.label` field (where empty → `REDACTED` and non-empty → `[REDACTED_<LABEL>]` per `redact.go:196-200`) is one table:\n\n| OPF label | `taggedRegion.label` | Rendered token |\n|---|---|---|\n| `private_person` | `PERSON` | `[REDACTED_PERSON]` |\n| `private_email` | `EMAIL` | `[REDACTED_EMAIL]` (matches existing regex PII) |\n| `private_phone` | `PHONE` | `[REDACTED_PHONE]` (matches existing regex PII) |\n| `private_address` | `ADDRESS` | `[REDACTED_ADDRESS]` (matches existing regex PII) |\n| `private_url` | `URL` | `[REDACTED_URL]` |\n| `private_date` | `DATE` | `[REDACTED_DATE]` |\n| `account_number` | `ACCOUNT_NUMBER` | `[REDACTED_ACCOUNT_NUMBER]` |\n| `secret` | `\"\"` (empty) | `REDACTED` (bare, matches betterleaks/entropy) |\n\nThe mapping table lives in `redact/opf.go` as a `map[string]string`. Falling through with a labeled token (rather than bare `REDACTED`) means a real human reading a redacted transcript can tell *why* something was redacted — names disappear as `[REDACTED_PERSON]` not anonymous `REDACTED` blobs, which makes \"is this over-redaction?\" feedback loops shorter.\n\n**One implementation footnote, not a design choice:** OPF returns spans against its tokenizer's view of the text. We need to verify whether the JSON output is character offsets or token offsets — the README says it decodes to \"coherent BIOES spans\" but doesn't pin down what the CLI emits. If it's tokens, we map them back to byte offsets at the adapter boundary. Calling this out for the data-flow section so we don't trip on it at implementation time.\n\n---\n\n### Section 4 — Progress UX and failure messages\n\nCondensation runs inside the PostCommit hook (`strategy/manual_commit_hooks.go`). Stdout/stderr from that hook is visible to the user's terminal — that's the channel we use. Three modes:\n\n**TTY, non-accessible (default):**\n```\n→ OpenAI Privacy Filter: scanning transcript…\n✓ OpenAI Privacy Filter: done (1.8s)\n```\nThe first line uses carriage-return clearing if you'd rather not leave it in scrollback; my recommendation is to **leave both lines** so the user always knows it ran. The \"done\" line acts as a receipt — important for an opt-in security feature.\n\n**Non-TTY (redirected, piped, CI):**\n```\n[entire] redaction: openai privacy filter scanning transcript (this may take a few seconds)\n[entire] redaction: openai privacy filter completed in 1.8s\n```\nSame content, structured prefix, no escape sequences.\n\n**`ACCESSIBLE=1`:**\nSame as non-TTY — plain text, no spinner, no carriage returns. Already the pattern for accessibility in the CLI per CLAUDE.md.\n\n**Failure messages — actionable, one line per cause:**\n\n| Failure | Message |\n|---|---|\n| Not on PATH | `× OpenAI Privacy Filter: 'opf' not found on PATH. Install with 'pip install opf' (see https://github.com/openai/privacy-filter) or set 'redaction.openai_privacy_filter.command' in .entire/settings.json. Falling back to default redaction layers.` |\n| Spawn failed (permission, etc.) | `× OpenAI Privacy Filter: failed to spawn '<command>': <err>. Falling back to default redaction layers.` |\n| Exceeded timeout | `× OpenAI Privacy Filter: exceeded <N>s timeout. Falling back to default redaction layers. Consider raising 'redaction.openai_privacy_filter.timeout_seconds' or disabling the filter.` |\n| Non-zero exit / garbled stdout | `× OpenAI Privacy Filter: <command> exited with status <N>. Falling back to default redaction layers. Last stderr: <truncated>` |\n| `on_failure: \"block\"` | Same diagnostic + `Condensation aborted because on_failure is set to 'block'. Edit .entire/settings.json to change.` |\n\nEvery message:\n- Names the feature unambiguously (`OpenAI Privacy Filter`, not `OPF`) so it's googleable\n- Says what failed and what the user can do about it\n- Says what happened to their data (`Falling back to default redaction layers.`) so trust is preserved\n- Goes to **stderr** so it doesn't corrupt any piped JSON output\n\nAll messages also go through `slog.Warn` via the existing `componentAttr = slog.String(\"component\", \"redaction\")` (from `custom.go:41`) so `entire doctor logs` shows them and `entire doctor bundle` includes them.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- The \"✓ done\" line matters more than the spinner. For a security-sensitive opt-in feature, the user wants to know it ran. Silent success looks identical to silent skip. One persistent line per condensation is cheap signal.\n- Mapping OPF spans into the existing `taggedRegion` merge machinery means we get conflict resolution for free — if entropy flags a 40-char hash and OPF flags the same hash as `secret`, the bigger region wins, no double substitution, no awkward `[REDACTED_SECRET]REDACTED` artifacts. Already-solved problem in `redact.go:235-265`.\n- Putting the install URL in the not-found message turns a friction point (developer hits an unfamiliar tool name) into a self-service path. Cheap to add, big UX win.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\nThree more sections left: data flow / callsite audit, testing strategy, and a quick package layout. Want me to keep going, or push back on anything in 3–4 first?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2601,"cache_read_input_tokens":136050,"output_tokens":2156,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_c