{"parentUuid":"b0f21ad8-6769-489d-aa24-b38fb7ef237c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_014XzJM8tvJcoHkMkB4EPspE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2159,"cache_read_input_tokens":138651,"output_tokens":4101,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2159,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":4101,"cache_read_input_tokens":138651,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2159,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2159},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cayej8o28aM1tSo2JgqTr","type":"assistant","uuid":"8b01ef93-c9d4-4833-ad92-d0787e277d2f","timestamp":"2026-05-13T00:51:10.511Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact","sessionId":"fb93c4d8-9548-4a4c-97e6-364245c50453","version":"2.1.140","gitBranch":"openai-redact"}
{"parentUuid":"8b01ef93-c9d4-4833-ad92-d0787e277d2f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_014XzJM8tvJcoHkMkB4EPspE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"### Section 5 — Data flow / callsite audit\n\nThe architecture in §1 introduced two entry-point pairs:\n\n```go\n// Plain (existing) — every per-turn write path.\nredact.String(s string) string\nredact.JSONLBytes(b []byte) (RedactedBytes, error)\n\n// New — augmented with OPF if configured.\nredact.StringWithPrivacyFilter(ctx context.Context, s string) string\nredact.JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter(ctx context.Context, b []byte) (RedactedBytes, error)\n```\n\nThe audit question: every existing `redact.JSONLBytes` / `redact.String` callsite needs to be classified as **per-turn** (stays on the plain entry point) or **condensation/export** (moves to the new entry point). Here's the full ledger from the earlier grep:\n\n| Callsite | Classification | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| `strategy/manual_commit_condensation.go:379` | **Condensation** — copies session → `entire/checkpoints/v1` | Switch to `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `strategy/manual_commit_hooks.go:2749` | **Condensation** — PostCommit transcript redaction | Switch to `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `strategy/manual_commit_hooks.go:2759` | **Condensation** — prompts redaction during PostCommit | Switch to `StringWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `checkpoint/committed.go:205, 264, 729, 1776` | **Condensation** — invoked transitively from condensation flow | Switch to `*WithPrivacyFilter` variants |\n| `checkpoint/committed.go:422, 840, 841, 859, 875, 1405` | **Condensation** — committed metadata fields (prompt, intent, outcome, classifications) | Switch to `StringWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `checkpoint/v2_committed.go:612, 906, 1018` | **Condensation (v2 path)** — same as committed.go | Switch to `*WithPrivacyFilter` variants |\n| `checkpoint/temporary.go:319, 390` | **Per-turn temp write** — shadow branch checkpoint | **Stays on plain** `JSONLBytes` |\n| `cli/attach.go:268` | **Export read** — reading stored transcript back out | Switch to `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `cli/explain.go:1111` | **Export read** — compacted transcript for explain | Switch to `JSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter` |\n| `cli/doctor_bundle.go:260, 266` | **Export bundle** — user is intentionally sharing | Switch to both `JSONLContent` and `Bytes` variants |\n| `cli/migrate.go:744, 1377, 1497, 1534` | **Migration** — `AlreadyRedacted` wrappers around persisted data | **No change** — already-redacted bytes don't get re-scanned |\n\n**The asymmetry made concrete:** temporary checkpoints get the fast pipeline (regex + entropy + betterleaks + custom + connection strings + credential values + opt-in regex PII). Condensation, export, and any path that produces bytes a human or remote system can see gets that PLUS OPF.\n\n**Bootstrapping (where OPF config is wired in):**\n`strategy/common.go` already wires the existing redact configurators:\n\n```go\n// Existing — strategy/common.go:373, 400\nredact.ConfigurePII(cfg)\nredact.ConfigureCustomRules(redact.CustomRulesConfig{ ... })\n```\n\nAdd a third sibling:\n\n```go\n// New\nif s.Redaction != nil && s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter != nil {\n    redact.ConfigurePrivacyFilter(redact.OPFConfig{\n        Enabled:        s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter.Enabled,\n        Categories:     s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter.Categories,\n        Command:        s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter.Command,\n        TimeoutSeconds: s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter.TimeoutSeconds,\n        OnFailure:      s.Redaction.OpenAIPrivacyFilter.OnFailure,\n    })\n}\n```\n\nSingle config call at startup; later calls to `*WithPrivacyFilter` read it through a guarded global the same way `getPIIConfig()` and `getCustomRulesConfig()` do today.\n\n---\n\n### Section 6 — Package layout\n\nOne new file in the `redact/` package plus one stand-alone runtime adapter. Keeps responsibilities crisp.\n\n```\nredact/\n├── redact.go                 (existing — regions, merge, JSONL machinery; +1 entry point pair)\n├── pii.go                    (existing — regex PII detector)\n├── custom.go                 (existing — user rules)\n├── packs.go                  (existing — rule packs)\n├── opf.go                    (NEW — OPF detector layer)\n└── opf_runtime/\n    ├── runtime.go            (NEW — interface: Redact(ctx, text, categories) → spans)\n    ├── shellout.go           (NEW v1 — exec.Command implementation)\n    └── daemon.go             (FUTURE — only if benchmark says daemon is worth it)\n```\n\n**`redact/opf.go`** holds the layer logic:\n- `OPFConfig` struct + `ConfigurePrivacyFilter`\n- `getOPFConfig()` (mirrors `getPIIConfig`)\n- The `detectOPF(ctx, cfg, s) []taggedRegion` function — called from inside the new `*WithPrivacyFilter` entry points\n- The OPF-label → `taggedRegion.label` mapping table from §3\n- A package-private `runtime` field of type `opf_runtime.Runtime` (interface) so we can swap shell-out → daemon later without changing `opf.go`\n\n**`redact/opf_runtime/runtime.go`** defines:\n```go\ntype Runtime interface {\n    Redact(ctx context.Context, text string, categories []string) ([]Span, error)\n    Close() error\n}\n\ntype Span struct {\n    Start int\n    End   int\n    Label string  // OPF's label, e.g. \"private_person\"\n}\n```\n\n**`redact/opf_runtime/shellout.go`** implements `Runtime` for v1 — spawns `opf` per call, talks JSON on stdin/stdout, respects context cancellation. ~100–150 LOC.\n\nA future `daemon.go` would implement the same interface using a unix-socket client, and we'd flip the constructor based on a settings field. **Zero changes to `redact/opf.go` or any callsite when we make that switch** — that's why the interface boundary exists.\n\n---\n\n### Section 7 — Testing strategy\n\nThree concentric rings, mapping onto how the existing redact package is tested:\n\n**Ring 1 — Unit tests in `redact/opf_test.go`** *(no `opf` binary required, runs in CI)*\n\nA fake `opf_runtime.Runtime` that returns canned spans. Verifies:\n- `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` correctly thresholds enabled/disabled\n- Disabled config → `detectOPF` returns nil with zero runtime calls\n- Span-to-`taggedRegion` mapping is correct for each of the 8 OPF labels\n- OPF regions merge correctly with regions from other layers (overlap with entropy hit, overlap with betterleaks hit, label-tie-break determinism)\n- Failure modes: runtime returns error → no panic, regions returned are nil, `on_failure: \"block\"` propagates; `\"warn\"` does not\n- Categories filter: if `private_person: true` but runtime returns a `private_email` span anyway, that span is dropped\n\n**Ring 2 — Adapter contract tests in `redact/opf_runtime/`** *(no `opf` binary required)*\n\nA stub binary in `testdata/fake-opf.sh` that the shell-out runtime invokes. Verifies the JSON-on-stdin-stdout protocol:\n- Request format we send is what we promise\n- Response format we accept is what `opf` documents\n- Timeout cancellation kills the process and returns context error\n- Non-zero exit / garbled JSON / empty stdout all become typed errors\n- The `command` setting overrides the binary path\n- Stderr from the child is captured into the error message\n\n**Ring 3 — Integration tests gated by `OPF_BIN` env var** *(skipped in CI by default)*\n\nMarked `//go:build opf_integration` so they only run when explicitly invoked:\n```go\nfunc TestOPF_LiveBinary(t *testing.T) {\n    if os.Getenv(\"OPF_BIN\") == \"\" {\n        t.Skip(\"set OPF_BIN to run live OPF integration tests\")\n    }\n    // …redact a real fixture; assert private_person spans match expected\n}\n```\n\nA small handful of these — enough to catch protocol drift if OPF upstream changes its output format. Devs with OPF installed can run them locally; CI doesn't pay the install/download cost.\n\n**Plus benchmark** — extend `redact_bench_test.go`:\n```go\nfunc BenchmarkRedactJSONLBytesWithPrivacyFilter(b *testing.B) {\n    // Same fixtures as existing benchmark.\n    // Uses fake runtime so we measure adapter overhead, not OPF.\n    // A separate //go:build opf_integration bench measures the real cost.\n}\n```\n\nThe fake-runtime bench gives us a stable \"OPF integration adds X ns of overhead\" number; the live bench gives us \"OPF adds Y seconds of wall time.\" Both useful, separated.\n\n---\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- The audit table is doing the load-bearing work for §1's architecture decision — without it, \"split the entry points\" sounds clean in the abstract but turns into \"did I migrate everything?\" in practice. Now we have an explicit list with a one-word classification each. Implementation is a checklist, not an exploration.\n- The Runtime interface boundary is the place where shell-out vs daemon decoupling lives. Everything north of that interface (config, detection, region mapping, merging, replacement) is the same in both worlds. If the benchmark says \"daemon,\" we add `daemon.go` and a constructor switch; nothing else moves.\n- The three-ring test structure mirrors how `redact/` is tested today (units everywhere, real `betterleaks` always-on because it's pure Go, no external runtime) but adds Ring 3 specifically because OPF is the first redaction layer with an external runtime. Without integration tests, \"the protocol still works\" rides on hope.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\nThat completes the design surface. Six sections:\n1. Architecture (two entry-point pairs, OPF as layer 8) ✅\n2. Settings schema (new `OpenAIPrivacyFilter` field under `RedactionSettings`) ✅\n3. Output format (OPF labels → existing token machinery) ✅\n4. Progress UX + failure messages (TTY/non-TTY/accessible; actionable warnings) ✅\n5. Data flow / callsite audit (every JSONLBytes call classified) ✅\n6. Package layout (`redact/opf.go` + `redact/opf_runtime/`) ✅\n7. Testing (three rings + benchmark, runtime-decision deferred to bench results) ✅\n\nReady to write this up as the formal design doc at `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-05-12-openai-privacy-filter-addon-design.md` and run the spec review loop? Or anything to push back on first?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2159,"cache_read_input_tokens":138651,"output_tokens":4101,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2159,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":4101,"cache_read_input_tokens":138651,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2159,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2159},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cayej8o28aM1tSo2JgqTr","type":"assistant","uuid":"043e7969-1839-4bc6-9c75-e8d5368d0ae7","timestamp":"2026-05-13T00:51:51.734Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact","sessionId":"fb93c4d8-9548-4a4c-97e6-364245c50453","version":"2.1.140","gitBranch":"openai-redact"}
