{"parentUuid":"c0ea7b77-ebca-4268-8fbd-844d7bc60163","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01Gyaed9CHm9Yprpw4hDXXmZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes — and the smoke test changes shape per chunk because what becomes observable changes. Here's the per-chunk plan, ordered by \"what can you actually see\":\n\n## What's observable after each chunk\n\n| Chunk | What works | What's invisible |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 (settings + config) | Config parses; `entire status` doesn't error | No detection. OPF is registered but never called. |\n| 2 (runtime adapter) | `opf` can be invoked from a small Go program | Still no detection in `redact.String*` |\n| 3 (detector + entry points + UX) | `redact.StringWithPrivacyFilter` works end-to-end with real `opf` | Real commits don't use it yet |\n| 4 (callsite migration) | Real condensation invokes OPF on every commit; transcripts on `entire/checkpoints/v1` contain `[REDACTED_PERSON]` | (this is the finish line) |\n\n## Per-chunk smoke tests\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- Chunks 1–2 are dark to the user, so their smoke tests are essentially \"did config plumbing work?\" rather than \"did redaction happen?\" The first chunk where you can *see* OPF redacting something is Chunk 3 — and it's a single Go test invocation, no commits required.\n- Chunk 4 is where the smoke test pays for itself, because that's where the existing condensation flow silently gains the new behavior. Doing the chunk-4 smoke test in a fresh test repo (not your main repo) keeps any over-redaction surprise contained.\n- The free regression check between every chunk is `mise run test:e2e:canary` — runs the Vogon agent, no API calls, no cost, validates that adding the new layer didn't break the existing pipeline.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n### After Chunk 1 — config parse only\n```bash\n# Write a settings file with OPF enabled but with a bogus command so\n# nothing will actually invoke it.\nmkdir -p /tmp/entire-opf-smoke && cd /tmp/entire-opf-smoke\ngit init -q\necho '{\"redaction\":{\"openai_privacy_filter\":{\"enabled\":true,\"categories\":{\"private_person\":true},\"command\":\"/nonexistent/opf\"}}}' > .entire/settings.json\n# Confirm settings load without errors:\nentire status   # should print status, no panic, no malformed-json warning\n# Then in your worktree:\nmise run test:e2e:canary  # regression check\n```\n**Pass criteria:** no panic, no settings-parse warning in `.entire/logs/entire.log`, canary passes.\n\n### After Chunk 2 — adapter wired to a real binary\n```bash\n# Requires opf installed: git clone openai/privacy-filter && pip install -e .\ngo test -tags opf_integration -run TestOPF_LiveBinary -v ./redact/opf_runtime/ -count=1\n```\nActually — the live test file lands in `redact/`, not `opf_runtime/`. After chunk 2 the only live thing in `opf_runtime/` is the shell-out adapter, so write a one-off probe:\n\n```bash\ncat > /tmp/opf_probe.go <<'EOF'\npackage main\nimport (\n    \"context\"\n    \"fmt\"\n    \"github.com/entireio/cli/redact/opf_runtime\"\n)\nfunc main() {\n    rt := opf_runtime.NewShellOut(\"opf\", 60)\n    spans, err := rt.Redact(context.Background(), \"Alice was born in 1990.\", []string{\"private_person\"})\n    fmt.Printf(\"spans=%+v err=%v\\n\", spans, err)\n}\nEOF\ngo run /tmp/opf_probe.go\n```\n**Pass criteria:** prints at least one span with `Label: \"private_person\"`.\n\n### After Chunk 3 — first time you can see redaction\nThis is the satisfying smoke test. No commits needed.\n\n```bash\n# Same opf install precondition.\ngo test -tags opf_integration -run TestOPF_LiveBinary_RedactsPerson -v ./redact/ -count=1\n```\nThe test is the one already defined in Task 10 Step 2 of the plan. It calls `StringWithPrivacyFilter` with a real `opf` binary and asserts `[REDACTED_PERSON]` appears.\n\nIf you want to drive it manually instead:\n```bash\ncat > /tmp/redact_probe.go <<'EOF'\npackage main\nimport (\n    \"context\"\n    \"fmt\"\n    \"github.com/entireio/cli/redact\"\n)\nfunc main() {\n    redact.ConfigurePrivacyFilter(redact.OPFConfig{\n        Enabled: true,\n        Categories: map[string]bool{\"private_person\": true},\n        Command: \"opf\",\n    })\n    got := redact.StringWithPrivacyFilter(context.Background(),\n        \"Alice was born in 1990 and lives in San Francisco.\")\n    fmt.Println(got)\n}\nEOF\ngo run /tmp/redact_probe.go\n```\n**Pass criteria:** stdout shows `[REDACTED_PERSON]` replacing \"Alice\", you see the `→ scanning transcript… / ✓ done (N.Ns)` UX lines on stderr.\n\n### After Chunk 4 — end-to-end against a test repo\nThis is the one where you watch OPF redact real commits. **Use a throwaway repo, not your main one** — if anything over-redacts, it's contained.\n\nThe repo has a `test-repo` skill exactly for this. Invoke `/test-repo` and pick the strategy validation flow, or do it manually:\n\n```bash\n# In a fresh test repo with opf installed:\nmkdir -p /tmp/opf-e2e && cd /tmp/opf-e2e\ngit init -q\necho '{\"redaction\":{\"openai_privacy_filter\":{\"enabled\":true,\"categories\":{\"private_person\":true},\"command\":\"opf\"}}}' > .entire/settings.json\nentire enable\n# Drive a session that mentions a name in chat with the agent. Then commit:\ngit commit --allow-empty -m \"test: trigger condensation\"\n# After the post-commit hook completes, look at the condensed transcript:\nentire explain HEAD --raw | grep -E \"(REDACTED_PERSON|Alice)\"\n```\n**Pass criteria:**\n- Stderr during commit shows `→ OpenAI Privacy Filter: scanning transcript… / ✓ done (N.Ns)`\n- The committed transcript on `entire/checkpoints/v1` has `[REDACTED_PERSON]` where names appeared in the agent's session\n- The benchmark numbers from Task 10 Step 6 should also be collected during this round so the daemon-vs-shellout decision data is in the PR\n\n## My recommendation\n\nRun between every chunk:\n```bash\nmise run test:e2e:canary   # ~1 min, free