lOut body ... noopRuntime is then deleted\"), which will prevent confusion when the next implementer lands. The `.golangci.yaml` addition is minimal and correctly scoped. Tests pass. Lint is clean.\n\n---\n\n**Issues:**\n\n**Critical:** None.\n\n**Important:**\n\n1. Tests omit `t.Parallel()` but the plan spec requires it and `CLAUDE.md` mandates it universally. The two tests in `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf_test.go` mutate the `opfConfig` global, which is the correct reason to omit `t.Parallel()`. However the existing codebase makes this omission explicit and intentional with a comment, and the plan's test snippet included `t.Parallel()` — which would be unsafe since both tests call `ConfigurePrivacyFilter` with no serialization. The implementation correctly dropped `t.Parallel()`, but the justification is not documented anywhere in the test file. `pii_test.go` at line 236 has a comment `// are NOT t.Parallel(). Keep this section small — detailed coverage / lives in Level 1...`. The OPF tests should carry an equivalent comment so future readers don't read `CLAUDE.md` and add `t.Parallel()` as a \"fix.\" This is the same pattern the plan deviated from — worth noting without blocking.\n\n2. The cleanup strategy in both tests uses `t.Cleanup(func() { ConfigurePrivacyFilter(OPFConfig{}) })` rather than directly zeroing the global. This leaves `opfConfig` pointing to a non-nil `*OPFConfig` with `Enabled: false` and all three defaults applied (Command `\"opf\"`, Timeout `30`, OnFailure `\"warn\"`). `pii_test.go` and `custom_test.go` both do a hard nil-reset (e.g. `piiConfig = nil`) to restore the pre-test \"never configured\" state. The divergence is not a current correctness problem because `detectOPF` (Task 4) will gate on `cfg.Enabled`, but it creates an inconsistency: `getOPFConfig() != nil` after cleanup, while `getPIIConfig() == nil`. If Task 4 adds any code that branches on `getOPFConfig() != nil` as a proxy for \"configured,\" the leaked default state will cause phantom OPF-path execution in tests that run after these ones. Consider a `resetOPFConfig` helper on the model of `resetPIIConfig` that directly sets `opfConfig = nil` under the lock, and use that in `t.Cleanup`.\n\n**Minor:**\n\n3. `ConfigurePrivacyFilterWithRuntime` duplicates the Timeout and OnFailure defaulting logic verbatim from `ConfigurePrivacyFilter`. The Command defaulting is intentionally absent (the test injector owns the runtime, so Command is irrelevant). This duplication is small (~6 lines) and the two functions will diverge further in Task 4 (production function may grow; test-seam function stays minimal), so extraction to a shared helper would add indirection for marginal gain. But it's worth a `//nolint:dupl` annotation if `mise run dup` flags it later, or a brief inline comment noting the intentional duplication to discourage naive DRY-refactoring.\n\n4. The `opf_runtime` package name uses an underscore, which `golangci-lint` doesn't flag in this project but is technically non-idiomatic Go (the spec says to use single-word package names or combine without separator). The `integration_test` directory uses `package integration` (drops the underscore). The underscore here is also the import path literal, which means callers write `opf_runtime.Runtime` and `opf_runtime.Span`. This is a cosmetic issue and not worth changing mid-stream (it would require updating `.golangci.yaml` and all import sites), but it is worth flagging so the team makes the naming decision consciously before this lands in main.\n\n5. `TestConfigurePrivacyFilter_StoresConfig` does not assert that `Categories` round-trips correctly (it sets `map[string]bool{\"private_person\": true}` but never reads it back). This is a trivial gap and the categories map is verified implicitly by the struct copy semantics, but adding `got.Categories[\"private_person\"] != true` would make the test's intent more explicit and catch any future fie