{"parentUuid":"26e52c25-32c1-455b-8192-b01c6987cf81","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"13e37c65-3ab6-4001-ab5c-7f3dac3ae5d6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0182NDv3mfteZz1hGoyQGg3w","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All the key surfaces have been examined. Here is the review.\n\n---\n\n**Strengths:**\n\nThe `CommandRunner` dependency-injection pattern is a faithful copy of the `ClaudeCodeAgent` shape: same field name (`CommandRunner`), same function signature (`func(ctx context.Context, name string, args ...string) *exec.Cmd`), same default assignment in the constructor (`exec.CommandContext`). Any future reader who knows the claudecode pattern will understand the seam immediately.\n\nError handling in `Redact` is well-layered. Timeout is detected via `callCtx.Err() == context.DeadlineExceeded`, the error wraps `callCtx.Err()` with `%w` so `errors.Is(err, context.DeadlineExceeded)` returns true, and the timeout duration is included in the message for actionability. Non-zero exit prefers `stderr` over the raw Go error and falls back gracefully when stderr is empty. JSON parse failure includes the raw stdout in the error message, which is exactly what you need when debugging a malformed opf output.\n\nThe early-return guard on `len(categories) == 0` is correct and prevents a degenerate `--labels \"\"` invocation from reaching the binary.\n\nThe `opf.go` caller sets `Timeout = 30` when `Timeout <= 0`, so `timeoutSeconds = 0` never reaches `shellOut` in production. The zero-timeout risk is mitigated at the call site rather than here, which is fine since `shellOut` is unexported.\n\n`Close() error` returning `nil` is appropriate. There is no persistent resource (no connection, no goroutine, no fd) to release per call. The plan's own spec shows the identical one-liner, and the `Runtime` interface requires `Close()` to be present for daemon compatibility in v2.\n\nThe `shCmd` helper in the test is a minor but pleasant improvement over the inline `exec.CommandContext(ctx, \"sh\", \"-c\", ...)` pattern in `claude_test.go`. It reduces repetition across five test cases without adding complexity.\n\nAll 6 tests pass cleanly and take 2 seconds total (dominated by the 1-second timeout test).\n\nLint reports 0 issues.\n\n---\n\n**Issues:**\n\nCritical: none.\n\nImportant:\n\n1. **Parent context cancellation produces a misleading error message.** If the caller cancels the parent `ctx` (not the internal `callCtx` timeout), `cmd.Run()` fails, but `callCtx.Err()` returns `context.Canceled` — not `context.DeadlineExceeded` — so the code falls through to `fmt.Errorf(\"opf exited with error: %s\", errMsg)`. Since `stderr` is empty on a signal-killed process, `errMsg` collapses to `cmd.Run()`'s error string (`\"signal: killed\"` or `\"context canceled\"`). The user sees \"opf exited with error: context canceled\" with no indication the parent context was the cause. A one-line fix:\n\n   In `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/redact/redact/opf_runtime/shellout.go`, replace the timeout-check block with:\n\n   ```go\n   if err := cmd.Run(); err != nil {\n       if callCtx.Err() == context.DeadlineExceeded {\n           return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"opf timeout after %s: %w\", timeout, callCtx.Err())\n       }\n       if ctx.Err() != nil {\n           return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"opf cancelled: %w\", ctx.Err())\n       }\n       // ... existing stderr passthrough\n   }\n   ```\n\n   This is not blocking for Chunk 2 in isolation (no production callsite drives cancellation yet), but should land before Chunk 3 when the real redact path is wired in.\n\n2. **`opfOutput` inline struct drops the `Text` field silently.** The `Text` field is parsed from OPF's JSON (`\"text\": \"Alice\"`) into the anonymous struct but is never mapped to `Span`. The public `Span` type has only `Start`, `End`, `Label`. If a future layer needs `Text` for display or audit, the omission will require a breaking change to `Span` or a parallel type. The plan's own spec says we consume character offsets, not `redacted_text`, so dropping `Text` from `Span` is deliberate. However, the anonymous struct capturing `Text` only to ignore it invites confusion. Either drop the `Text` field from the anonymous struct entirely (it unmarshals fine without it — Go ignores unknown fields by default) or add a comment explaining the deliberate discard. Omitting the field is cleaner:\n\n   ```go\n   type opfOutput struct {\n       DetectedSpans []struct {\n           Label string `json:\"label\"`\n           Start int    `json:\"start\"`\n           End   int    `json:\"end\"`\n           // Text field from OPF wire format is intentionally not captured;\n           // callers reconstruct text from input using Start/End offsets.\n       } `json:\"detected_spans\"`\n   }\n   ```\n\nMinor:\n\n1. **`Close()` has no doc comment.** Every other exported method on an interface implementation typically gets one. Given `shellOut` is unexported, this is low stakes, but the `Runtime` interface doc already says \"must be safe for concurrent use\" without clarifying `Close` semantics. A one-line comment (`// Close is a no-op for the shell-out runtime; there is no persistent resource to release.`) makes the intent obvious when someone adds a daemon implementation and wonders if they need to call `Close()`.\n\n2. **`shCmd` helper function is non-idiomatic relative to the established pattern.** The `claude_test.go` inlines `exec.CommandContext(ctx, \"sh\", \"-c\", ...)` directly. The `shCmd` helper is not wrong — it reduces duplication and is readable — but it is a divergence from the reference pattern the plan cited. The plan explicitly says \"No bash stub, no `testdata/` executable.\" It does not prohibit a named helper. Leave it as-is.\n\n3. **The `sync` import is present in the test file solely for `TestShellOut_CategoriesPassed`.** The mutex is correctly used (value-type `sync.Mutex` captured by 