{"parentUuid":"ab8598eb-68d1-4852-9ca5-233995d12648","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01KNidvJ9VCn8vdg7hoGXjYx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Honest answer: **only F and G actually show the user UX.** A/B/C/D/E are diagnostic — they prove the code works but don't represent anything a user would ever do.\n\n## What an Entire user actually sees\n\nThe user-facing experience has exactly three moments:\n\n### 1. Editing `.entire/settings.json` (one-time setup)\n\nUser adds:\n```json\n{\n  \"redaction\": {\n    \"openai_privacy_filter\": {\n      \"enabled\": true,\n      \"categories\": {\"private_person\": true},\n      \"command\": \"opf\",\n      \"on_failure\": \"warn\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNothing visible happens after saving. They keep working.\n\n### 2. Running `git commit` (every time)\n\nThis is **the** user moment. The post-commit hook runs condensation, OPF fires, stderr shows:\n\n```\n[main 4f3a92c] my normal commit message\n→ OpenAI Privacy Filter: scanning transcript…\n✓ OpenAI Privacy Filter: done (1.8s)\n 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)\n```\n\nOr, if `opf` isn't installed and they have it enabled:\n\n```\n[main 4f3a92c] my normal commit message\n× OpenAI Privacy Filter: 'opf' not found on PATH. Install with 'pip install opf' (see https://github.com/openai/privacy-filter) or set 'redaction.openai_privacy_filter.command' in .entire/settings.json. Falling back to default redaction layers.\n 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)\n```\n\nThat's it. The commit completes either way. The user-facing surface during the commit is **two lines of stderr** (or one for failure).\n\n### 3. Inspecting condensed transcripts (occasional)\n\nWhen the user runs `entire explain HEAD` or `entire explain HEAD --raw`, they see the redacted transcript. Anywhere their agent or session mentioned a person's name:\n\n```json\n{\"role\":\"user\",\"message\":\"Add a comment with my name Alice Smith on it\"}\n```\n\nbecomes:\n\n```json\n{\"role\":\"user\",\"message\":\"Add a comment with my name [REDACTED_PERSON] on it\"}\n```\n\n## The single smoke that shows all three moments\n\nSmoke F is the one. Run it and you'll see:\n- The progress lines during `git commit` (moment #2 — visible in your terminal)\n- The redacted transcript via `entire explain HEAD --raw` (moment #3 — printed by the script)\n\nIf you also want to see the failure path (moment #2 alternate), repeat F with the `command` set to `/nonexistent/opf` and watch the `×` line appear during the commit.\n\n## What F doesn't show that G does\n\nF uses Vogon — a deterministic fake agent. The \"transcript\" content is whatever Vogon parses out of the prompt (in our case, the `Hello from Alice Smith…` string). It's a real commit and real condensation, but the prose is synthetic.\n\nG uses a real agent (claude-code, gemini-cli, etc.). The transcript content is whatever the LLM actually generated during a real session. That's the **truest** \"what users see\" — natural prose flowing through your terminal with the progress lines, and an `entire explain` that has the kind of unpredictable wording a real agent produces.\n\n## My honest recommendation\n\nIf you just want one smoke that shows \"what a user sees,\" **run F**. Then mentally substitute \"real agent prose\" for Vogon's output and that's your user experience.\n\nIf you want the truly authentic user experience, **run G** — same setup but use `--agent claude-code` (or whichever you have configured) with a prompt that mentions names.\n\nSkip A/B/C/D/E for \"user UX\" purposes — they're protocol-level diagnostics for confirming the wiring works."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3945,"cache_read_input_tokens":610899,"output_tokens":2975,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3945,"ep